How Foam Works with Git, GitHub, and GitLens
Based on my research, here's the complete picture:
Foam's Core Git/GitHub Integration
Foam is fundamentally built on Visual Studio Code and GitHub, combining these tools where GitHub provides version control, backup, and collaboration capabilities 
Foam
. This is actually a foundational design choice, not an add-on.
The Setup: The recommended workflow starts by creating a GitHub repository from Foam's template, which you can make private if you want to keep your thoughts to yourself, then cloning it locally and opening it in VS Code 
GitHub
. Every note you write becomes a version-controlled markdown file.
Key Benefits for Fiction Writing:
Version History for Your Story - Every edit to your characters, plot notes, or world-building is tracked. You can see how your ideas evolved over time, revert bad changes, or compare different versions of a character concept.
Backup & Sync - Your entire fictional universe is backed up to GitHub automatically. Work on your novel notes from multiple devices seamlessly.
Branching for Alternate Storylines - Want to explore a different plot direction without losing your current work? Create a Git branch. Test out killing off that character in a branch before committing to it in your main storyline.
Collaboration - If you're co-writing or working with an editor, they can fork your repository, suggest changes via pull requests, and you can review/merge their edits.
